`main` is a live, moving branch that interleaves post-MVP proposal files
(GC, exceptions, tail calls, SIMD, threads/atomics) with plain WASM 1.0
MVP-core ones, so a pinned SHA -- not `main` itself -- is vendored to keep
this repo's conformance baseline reproducible across re-fetches. Bumping
the pin is a deliberate, reviewed decision, not something a re-run of the
fetch script does casually.

Mostly WASM 1.0 MVP-core `.wast` files are vendored here; see
`code/specs/W05-wasm-conformance-harness.md` section 6 for the original
exclusion rationale (SIMD, threads, exceptions, tail calls, bulk memory
operations, GC/reference-types beyond this repo's existing narrow slice,
memory64, the component model, and files needing the `spectest`
host-import module) -- still the same list after the WASM08 slice
extension, just a larger set of files that satisfy it. WASM18 (`code/
specs/W09-wasm-atomics-plain.md`) is the first deliberate exception:
`atomic.wast` exercises PLAIN atomic memory ops (load/store/RMW/
cmpxchg/fence), which don't need real multi-threading to be meaningful
-- `memory.atomic.notify`/`wait32`/`wait64`, in that same file, remain
genuinely out of scope and are graded `NotYetSupported`. Every other
excluded category above still becomes its own future phase's vendored
slice.

`linking.wast` (task #93) is a second deliberate exception: real cross-
module linking (imports/exports/`register` across separate `WasmInstance`s)
has had genuine support since WASM05, so the file's own original W05
exclusion note ("heavier module-linking semantics") no longer applies. Of
its 71 modules, exactly 2 import from `spectest` (this crate still has no
`spectest` host, by design) -- those two modules and whatever directives
depend on them grade `NotYetSupported`/collateral-fail; every other
directive exercises real, already-supported linking machinery.

