`simd_bitwise.wast` (task #150-152) covers `v128.not`/`and`/`andnot`/
`or`/`xor`/`bitselect` -- the lane-width-agnostic raw-byte bitwise
family, unlike every prior `simd_*` file which was scoped to one lane
width. Represents a strategic pivot from "widen the next narrow
per-lane-width family" (the pattern used in every prior SIMD PR) to
"close the highest-real-world-impact remaining gap", identified via a
broader prioritization survey now that `i8x16`/`i16x8`/`i32x4` all have
complete arith+cmp+arith2+widening coverage relative to each other.
`bitselect` is the first TERNARY SIMD op implemented in this
interpreter (pops three `v128`s, pushes one): `(a AND c) OR (b AND
(NOT c))`, i.e. select bits from `a` where the corresponding `c` bit is
1, else from `b`. Each sub-opcode byte fetched live from `BinarySIMD.md`
and cross-checked against the already-implemented `i8x16.add`/
`i32x4.add` entries, same discipline as every prior addition. 100%
pass on every gradeable directive kind (1/1 modules, 126/126
assert_return, 28/28 assert_invalid; 13 assert_return directives grade
`NotYetSupported` because they depend on `v128.load`, not a real
failure).

